    "metadata": {},
    "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6",
    "object": "response",
    "output": [
      {
        "encrypted_content": "kpp:reasoning:v1:",
        "id": "rs_kaeso_msg011CfvjgR4tvemLvtmGDn8Wd_0",
        "summary": [
          {
            "text": "Yes, this is a classical theorem in number theory. There are infinitely many primes of the form 4k + 3 (i.e., primes p where p ≡ 3 (mod 4)).\n\nThis is proven by a classic proof similar to Euclid's proof of infinitely many primes.\n\n**Proof:**\n\nAssume there are finitely many primes congruent to 3 mod 4: p₁, p₂, ..., pₙ.\n\nConsider N = 4·p₁·p₂·...·pₙ - 1 = 4(p₁·p₂·...·pₙ - 1) + 3.\nSo N ≡ 3 (mod 4). Since a product of factors all ≡ 1 (mod 4) would itself be ≡ 1 (mod 4), N must have at least one prime factor q ≡ 3 (mod 4). This q can't equal any of the pᵢ, since N leaves remainder -1 when divided by each pᵢ.\n\nSo q is a new prime ≡ 3 (mod 4), contradicting our finite list — hence there are infinitely many such primes. This result is also a special case of Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions.\n\n",
            "type": "summary_text"
          }
        ],
        "type": "reasoning"
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "annotations": [],
            "logprobs": [],
            "text": "## Yes — There Are Infinitely Many Primes ≡ 3 (mod 4)\n\nThis is a classical result, provable with an argument similar to Euclid's proof of infinite primes.\n\n---\n\n### Proof by Contradiction\n\n**Assume** there are only finitely many such primes: $p_1, p_2, \\ldots, p_k$, all satisfying $p_i \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$.\n\n**Construct** the number:\n$$N = 4p_1 p_2 \\cdots p_k - 1$$\n\nNote that $N \\equiv 3 \\pmod 4$, and $N$ is odd (so 2 is not a factor).\n\n**Key observation:** The product of numbers each $\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}$ is itself $\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}$.\n\nSince $N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$, **at least one** prime factor $q$ of $N$ must satisfy $q \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$.\n\n**But:** $q$ cannot be any $p_i$, because:\n$$p_i \\mid 4p_1\\cdots p_k \\quad \\text{and} \\quad p_i \\mid N \\implies p_i \\mid 1 \\quad \\text{(impossible)}$$\n\nThis **contradicts** our assumption that the list was complete. $\\square$\n\n---\n\n### Examples of such primes:\n$$3, 7, 11, 19, 23, 31, 43, 47, 59, 67, \\ldots$$\n\nThis result also follows as a special case of **Dirichlet's theorem**, which guarantees infinitely many primes in any arithmetic progression $a + nd$ where $\\gcd(a, d) = 1$ — here $a=3,\\ d=4$.",
            "type": "output_text"
          }
        ],
        "id": "msg_kaeso_msg011CfvjgR4tvemLvtmGDn8Wd_1",
        "role": "assistant",
        "status": "completed",
        "type": "message"
      }
